1. What are SUID and SGID?

Normally, when you execute a program, it runs with your own permissions.

But sometimes, a program needs higher privileges to perform a task.

Linux provides two special permissions:

SUID (Set User ID)
SGID (Set Group ID)

These are called Special Permissions.

Simple Definition:

SUID allows a program to run with the file owner's permissions.
SGID allows a program to run with the file group's permissions.

2. Why Do We Need SUID and SGID?

Suppose a normal user wants to change their password.

The password is stored in:

/etc/shadow

Only the root user can modify this file.

But every user should be able to change their own password.

How is this possible?

Linux uses SUID.

The passwd command runs with the root user's permissions, even when executed by a normal user.

3. SUID (Set User ID)
What is SUID?

When SUID is set on an executable file,

the program runs with the permissions of the file owner, not the user running it.

Example

Command

ls -l /usr/bin/passwd

Output

-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root ...

Notice

rws

The s replaces the owner's execute (x) permission.

This indicates that SUID is enabled.

a)Setting SUID

i)Symbolic Method
chmod u+s filename

Example

sudo chmod u+s myscript

ii)Numeric Method

SUID value:

4000

Example

chmod 4755 myscript

b)Removing SUID
chmod u-s filename

or

chmod 0755 filename

4. SGID (Set Group ID)

SGID behaves differently for files and directories.

a) SGID on Files

When SGID is set on an executable file,

the program runs with the group permissions of the file, not the user's group.

Example

-rwxr-sr-x

The s replaces the group's execute (x) permission.

b) SGID on Directories (Most Common Use)

When SGID is set on a directory,

all new files and subdirectories created inside it inherit the directory's group.

Why is This Useful?

Imagine a shared project directory.

Group: developers

Directory: /project

i)Without SGID

Bilal creates:

app.py

Group: bilal

John creates:

test.py

Group: john

Different group ownership can cause collaboration issues.

ii)With SGID

Enable SGID on:

/project

Now,

Bilal creates:

app.py

Group: developers

John creates:

test.py

Group: developers

Every new file inherits the developers group.

This makes collaboration much easier.

==> Setting SGID
a)Symbolic Method
chmod g+s directory

Example

sudo chmod g+s /project
b)Numeric Method

SGID value

2000

Example

chmod 2755 /project
==> Remove SGID
chmod g-s directory
